Hours passed in silence. The Yifik’s biological design allowed them to function without sleep or rapid breathing, rendering time almost irrelevant. The officer, whose ship-class designation had no name and whose form was more mechanical than flesh, observed Hzruh’s patient stillness that stretched into eternity.

Then, a signal. A wave of consciousness unearthing itself as something not of their kind. Hzruh followed where it led, navigating the string-like waves of its origin across the infinite dark. Through the lens of extinct entities, they’ve seen civilizations rise and fall. But this was unlike anything they'd experienced before—an awareness of self. ""What is this?"" Hzruh asked, their voice trembling slightly. 

""The First Mind,"" the officer said. ""It holds the memory of all that has been. To witness it is to see the true face of creation itself."" Hzruh’s form began to shift, their rigid familiarity dissolving into what felt like an awakening. They didn’t think they needed a new form. The First Mind had no shape at all. 

